This looks like January 2018. The fed funds rate is about 1.42%, which fits the range after the December 2017 hike. The 2-year yield at 2.13% and the 10-year at 2.73% also fit that period, since the 10-year rose in January 2018. Unemployment is 4.1%, core CPI is 1.77% and the dollar is down 7.9% year over year, all consistent with early 2018. US equities returned 26% over 12 months with the VIX at 14.79, which suggests the data end in January before the February selloff.

The 1-month equity return of +5.6% matches January 2018, when the S&P 500 gained about 5.7%. Emerging-market equities rose 8.3% that month, which also fits.

